INT. FAMILY DINING ROOM - SUNDAY DINNER

A long table, a good cloth, a roast chicken half carved. Two places set. Six empty chairs. Steam rises from a gravy boat nobody has touched.

BEATRICE MONTGOMERY-AKINYEMI (60s, pearls, apron over her Sunday dress) carves with unnecessary force. Across the table, her son THEODORE "TEDDY" MONTGOMERY-AKINYEMI (30s, tie loosened, jaw tight) stirs his peas.

BEATRICE
Everyone's welcome to begin.

TEDDY
Mama, there's no one else.

BEATRICE
(to the room)
Then no one shall be offended.

She serves him a slice. Sets the platter down. Sits.

BEATRICE (CONT'D)
Your father always said grace.

TEDDY
I'm aware.

BEATRICE
Someone should.

TEDDY
Fine. Bless this food.

BEATRICE
Amen.

They eat. Forks clink. The clock ticks.

BEATRICE (CONT'D)
(to herself)
Too dry.

TEDDY
It's fine.

BEATRICE
It is not fine. I overcooked it.

TEDDY
Mama.

BEATRICE
I overcooked it because I was thinking about your sister.

Teddy sets down his fork.

TEDDY
Don't.

BEATRICE
Eight years, Theodore. Eight years she has not sat in that chair.

TEDDY
She has her reasons.

BEATRICE
She has a grudge.

TEDDY
She has a reason!

His voice cracks across the table. Beatrice flinches. A pause. Teddy pushes back his chair.

TEDDY (CONT'D)
You want to know why she doesn't come? Because every Sunday you set six empty places and call it hope.

BEATRICE
It is hope.

TEDDY
It's a stage set!

BEATRICE
How dare you.

TEDDY
How dare I?

He stands. The cloth drags. Gravy slops.

BEATRICE
Sit down.

TEDDY
No.

BEATRICE
Sit down, Theodore.

TEDDY
I'm thirty-four years old.

He walks out. The front door slams. Beatrice sits alone, staring at the empty chairs.
